# ===========================================================================
# LINE ENDINGS ARE A CORRECTNESS PROPERTY IN THIS TREE, NOT A PREFERENCE.
# ===========================================================================
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, measured, and it cost an hour:
#
# cmd.exe parses a batch file by BYTE OFFSET while counting the text it has
# consumed in CHARACTERS.  With CRLF the two happen to stay in step.  With bare
# LF plus ONE multi-byte character anywhere in the file they drift, and the
# parser resumes MID-LINE, SOMEWHERE ELSE ENTIRELY.  Adding one Chinese
# sentence to verify.bat made cmd execute `set "R' and then try to run
# `C_REL_BUILD=0' as a program -- at line 56, a hundred and sixty lines above
# the edit, while still printing banner [1/6].
#
# The truth table was measured four ways: BOTH conditions must hold.  LF alone
# is harmless.  Multi-byte alone is harmless.  LF AND multi-byte mis-executes.
#
# tools\lint-door-coverage.ps1 (Test-BatchFileHygiene) guards BOTH halves of
# that conjunction, by reading BYTES.  THIS FILE IS THE OTHER HALF OF THE SAME
# GUARANTEE: the lint says "the .bat in your working tree is well formed", and
# this file says "the .bat git puts in your working tree is well formed in the
# first place".  Neither is sufficient alone -- a lint cannot fix a checkout,
# and a checkout rule cannot see an edit.
#
# AND THE .bat HALF PROTECTS THE GATE ITSELF.  The lint runs from inside
# verify.bat's :lint_doors, so a verify.bat that has ALREADY drifted
# mis-executes before it ever reaches the check.  A checker cannot be the only
# thing guarding the file that invokes it; that is why the rule lives here, in
# something git applies before any of our code runs.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW TO READ IT: later lines win, so this file goes broad -> narrow.
# `eol=` is spelled out everywhere rather than left to core.autocrlf, because
# core.autocrlf is a PER-MACHINE setting and the whole point here is that the
# working tree is the same bytes on every machine.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The default.  Anything git recognises as text: LF in the index, LF in the
# working tree.  The index in this repository was already uniformly LF before
# this file existed, so this line changes no committed byte -- it only stops
# the WORKING TREE from drifting away from it, which it already had (twenty-odd
# files were checked out CRLF and eight of them were internally MIXED).
* text=auto eol=lf

# The library's own sources, stated explicitly rather than left to the
# text=auto heuristic.  That heuristic decides text-vs-binary by looking for NUL
# bytes, and one string literal with an embedded NUL would silently take a
# translation unit out of normalisation and let it drift.  These are the
# primary asset; they do not get to depend on a guess.
*.c    text eol=lf
*.cc   text eol=lf
*.cpp  text eol=lf
*.h    text eol=lf
*.hpp  text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CRLF, BECAUSE THE INTERPRETER REQUIRES IT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# cmd.exe.  See the header.  This is the line REQ-40 is about.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf

# Windows PowerShell 5.1.  CRLF is the native convention for the interpreter
# these scripts hard-code the path of
# (%SystemRoot%\System32\WindowsPowerShell\v1.0\powershell.exe), and two of
# these files already carry a UTF-8 BOM because 5.1 needs one to read non-ASCII
# correctly -- so they are Windows-only artefacts by construction.
#
# It also hardens the tree's own documented remediation: verify.bat tells you
# that when a message needs non-ASCII text you MOVE IT INTO A .ps1.  That
# advice sends multi-byte characters into these files on purpose, so these
# files are exactly where the substrate should be the safe one.
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
*.psm1 text eol=crlf
*.psd1 text eol=crlf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BYTES THAT ARE THE TEST SUBJECT.  NOT NORMALISED, EVER.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The ASan classifier's fixtures.  Its sentinel case is
# third-party-sogou-uaf-stock-thunk.log, whose line 18 is deliberately GBK
# while every other fixture's matching line is UTF-8 -- "the shape matches, the
# bytes do not" is the property under test.  Normalising a fixture does not
# fail a test, it DELETES the test's subject and leaves it green, which is the
# worst of the two directions.
#
# `-text` (never convert) rather than `text eol=lf` (convert to LF), because
# the requirement here is not "LF", it is "the bytes I committed".  `diff` is
# set alongside so `-text` does not also cost us readable diffs: these stay
# reviewable in git log, they just stop being rewritable by git.
tests/data/asan/** -text diff

# The visual-regression baselines.  Nine PNGs, compared BYTE FOR BYTE by
# tests/visual/test_golden.cpp; normalising one is not a test failure, it is
# the visual gate silently switching off.  git's heuristic already classifies
# them binary today -- this line means it no longer has to be right about that.
*.png binary

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FIXTURES THAT MUST STAY BROKEN
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `*.bat text eol=crlf` above is repository-wide, and it would reach into
# tools\test-lint-door-coverage.ps1's fixture tree and REPAIR the two files
# whose whole job is to be defective.  A negative fixture that git silently
# fixes on checkout is a test that passes forever without testing anything --
# the same failure mode as normalising a golden, one level down.
#
# So they are pinned, individually and by name.  Two lines, not a directory
# glob: every OTHER .bat under tests/data/lint/ is a fixture-root verify.bat
# that must be WELL formed (its case expects a green lint), and a blanket
# exemption would quietly stop covering those.
tests/data/lint/bat-nonascii/armed.bat text eol=lf
tests/data/lint/bat-eol-lf/hygiene.bat text eol=lf
